[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064050 :question/id #uuid "66cbcaeb-93ee-5c15-a230-e66d2fbdd761" :question/math-academy-id "e-380" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int -6(7^x) \\, \\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constant $-6.$ Then, we integrate the exponential function, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int (-6)7^x  \\, \\textrm d x   \n&= -6\\int 7^x\\,\\textrm{d}x\\\\\n&=-6 \\cdot \\dfrac{7^x}{\\ln 7} + C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064054 :question/id #uuid "3d2bde25-f8dc-5e62-9999-6593424f0ae2" :question/math-academy-id "e-384" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = \\tan x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm dx = \\sec x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\int (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x& = 2 \\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x+ \\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=2\\tan x + \\sec x + C.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064097 :question/id #uuid "74761123-d539-5d78-8dcd-4ea1db04bed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-486" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division. \n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\dfrac{ 2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3} .\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064102 :question/id #uuid "6e148f32-5352-5368-a08a-6da278a6068d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5124" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} = ay + b,$  what are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= \\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2y - \\dfrac 1 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(2)}_{a}y+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{1}{3})}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064103 :question/id #uuid "41294f0b-dc6a-5a84-a7f8-7c2370b5733c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5125" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} = p + qa + rb,$ what are the values of $p,$ $q,$ and $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{4})}_{p}+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{1}{2})}_{q}a+\\underbrace{(4)}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064119 :question/id #uuid "5aa1e9b2-a6d3-507d-8530-9cbba2fc2162" :question/math-academy-id "e-6707" :question/problem "Simplify the product $(x - 3)(x^2 - 2x + 6).$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute both terms of the binomial to the trinomial in the second parenthesis. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x - 3)(x^2 - 2x + 6) &= x(x^2 - 2x + 6) - 3(x^2 - 2x + 6) \\\\ &= x^3 - 2x^2 + 6x - 3x^2 + 6x - 18 \\\\ &= x^3 - 5x^2 + 12x - 18\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064120 :question/id #uuid "55f28c00-cfe7-507e-830f-f7d993d16687" :question/math-academy-id "e-6710" :question/problem "Given that  $(3s^2 + 2s - 1)(s^2 + s + 2) = 3s^4 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{a}s^3 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{b}s^2 + \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{c}s - 2,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{s}^{2}+2s-1)({s}^{2}+s+2) &= 3{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+s+2)+2s({s}^{2}+s+2)+(-1)({s}^{2}+s+2) \\\\ &= 3{s}^{4}+3{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+2{s}^{3}+2{s}^{2}+4s-{s}^{2}-s-2 \\\\ &= 3{s}^{4}+5{s}^{3}+7{s}^{2}+3s-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMatching up the coefficients, we find that $a=5,$ $b=7,$ and $c=3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na + b + c = 5 + 7 + 3 = 15.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064121 :question/id #uuid "9e77dab4-4e8b-51df-a070-1a42fcdbf212" :question/math-academy-id "e-6711" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $a^2b$ in the product $(a+b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}) &= a({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2})+b({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}) \\\\ &= {a}^{3}+{a}^{2}b+a{b}^{2}+{a}^{2}b+a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\ &= {a}^{3}+2{a}^{2}b+2a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $a^2 b$ is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f956e5b-1886-5c73-b8bc-cf07f5514c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6w+1)(w+10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482836} {:answer/id #uuid "139e2599-d442-5bcf-9fed-9beb770bdac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+5)(2w+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482846} {:answer/id #uuid "47b0fd5d-4c37-52f2-9295-22a8f59254c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3w+1)(w+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482940} {:answer/id #uuid "a8505418-0dab-57f8-904d-143d5e551a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+10)(2w+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483097} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd19af-9737-539e-b11a-593a8a2fa762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+2)(2w+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd19af-9737-539e-b11a-593a8a2fa762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+2)(2w+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483189} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5de56ee-fdfa-5ee6-87f8-0226508bf6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acf95ff0-c23c-5538-9064-3e039f763a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-111835" :question/problem "$6{w}^{2}+19w+10=$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n6{w}^{2}+19w+10.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($6\\times 10=60$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($19$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $60.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $60$ are\n\n$$\n(1,60),\\,(2,30),\\,(3,10),\\,(4,15),\\,(5,12),\\,(6,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(4,15)$ add to $19:$ \n\n$$\n4+15=19\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $4w+15w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+19w+10 &= 6{w}^{2}+4w+15w+10 \\\\ &= (6{w}^{2}+4w)+(15w+10) \\\\ &= 2w(3w+2)+5(3w+2) \\\\ &= (3w+2)(2w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a9d02b-b538-5620-b733-61af6ed20e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a9d02b-b538-5620-b733-61af6ed20e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544108} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde59380-e255-5942-b3f7-64c440563de2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda4f1f8-6966-5ad9-a7a5-567211e5a9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dda4f1f8-6966-5ad9-a7a5-567211e5a9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544566} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6cbc592-2c25-5ac2-98cd-00aabe81a7d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3001dc-c422-5f38-bf1a-6e6aac3baf6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a3001dc-c422-5f38-bf1a-6e6aac3baf6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544141} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed63e6cb-39c3-5d3f-a8c6-4ef7473abfa3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e011b39f-8f95-5783-bae7-e146d27b9c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-213910" :question/problem "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$2{x}^{2}+7x+6=(x+\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x+6.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times 6=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(2,6),\\,(3,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(3,4)$ add to $7:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+4 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $3x+4x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+6 &= 2{x}^{2}+3x+4x+6 \\\\ &= (2{x}^{2}+3x)+(4x+6) \\\\ &= x(2x+3)+2(2x+3) \\\\ &= (x+2)(2x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a3f9bc-2830-5b92-931a-3c810b62441b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14a3f9bc-2830-5b92-931a-3c810b62441b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480774} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e63775-9bbf-5bac-9ed9-630837fc923d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272b01ca-2905-5fd4-8795-37ff6fdbfcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "272b01ca-2905-5fd4-8795-37ff6fdbfcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480811} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b24c1f-e0a9-5e81-80d0-15c9916d9962" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f1bfe9-db04-5089-b55e-98e9532c2a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f1bfe9-db04-5089-b55e-98e9532c2a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481046} :answer-field/id #uuid "20f982b0-71a4-5ef7-924d-0be8af36f72d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061692} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402fdbb8-b6b5-5849-b4b5-7740744567bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "402fdbb8-b6b5-5849-b4b5-7740744567bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480863} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f3ab51-ce5a-5bd3-bd7d-e65ca8d3a9a1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a603bcbe-594a-560c-9760-7b1c995efc5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-216734" :question/problem "$(7{u}^{2}-6v)(v-u)=${{field-1}}${u}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${u}^{2}v\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$uv\\,-$\n{{field-4}}${v}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7{u}^{2}-6v)(v-u) &= 7{u}^{2}(v-u)+(-6v)(v-u) \\\\ &= 7{u}^{2}v-7{u}^{3}-6{v}^{2}+6uv \\\\ &= -7{u}^{3}+7{u}^{2}v+6uv-6{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f9fb81-1660-5d2f-8367-cb9e54b394f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481144} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2673aa-48dc-51fb-bef1-5108cb413aa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b6f2199-f93d-54e4-a4b1-9693a8b2262c" :question/math-academy-id "q-217025" :question/problem "$2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3})=${{field-1}}\n$\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${b}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) &= 2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{4})+2{b}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}+2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ &= (-\\frac{1}{2})({b}^{3+1})+2({b}^{3+3})+(-\\frac{2}{3}){b}^{3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}+2{b}^{6}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3} \\\\ &= 2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d12287-da2e-5723-9891-def9ab5606f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d12287-da2e-5723-9891-def9ab5606f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482982} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c88bae-0e5d-514c-bf54-54c3a911e7e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd80e3a-05e9-5e40-a54c-e5cfea2ecc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd80e3a-05e9-5e40-a54c-e5cfea2ecc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483034} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8e7b90-bd74-5dd6-b27c-58a4dbac0a72" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3190ce8-8d61-5d10-b6da-2e9aeb1500b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3190ce8-8d61-5d10-b6da-2e9aeb1500b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483168} :answer-field/id #uuid "f707b313-e9c4-51d8-ae46-03c5b10920ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32c14ac4-d421-5d0c-b8af-4d8855fd2e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-224920" :question/problem "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$4{x}^{2}-15x+9=(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-15x+9.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($4\\times 9=36$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $36.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $36$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-36),\\,(-2,-18),\\,(-3,-12),\\,(-4,-9),\\,(-6,-6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-3,-12)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)+(-12) & =-15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-3x-12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-15x+9 &= 4{x}^{2}-3x-12x+9 \\\\ &= (4{x}^{2}-3x)-(12x-9) \\\\ &= x(4x-3)-3(4x-3) \\\\ &= (x-3)(4x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2198ba-923e-5056-b8e4-6d9278cbada9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480800} {:answer/id #uuid "55a60124-b85f-5634-bcc9-5b892c5317c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{q}^{2}+8q+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480895} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5aa05e-a354-5093-8e3c-d00252beb5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9q+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480941} {:answer/id #uuid "939e84cc-3e36-5e09-a1b0-85393d657a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{q}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481017} {:answer/id #uuid "9c32ca25-8e1f-5a20-8a20-18245e0893d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{q}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a60124-b85f-5634-bcc9-5b892c5317c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{q}^{2}+8q+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480895} :answer-field/id #uuid "794ffb6c-be6f-58a9-8839-947f1a065b9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb6f6c86-b958-598c-9c38-c9ef7841d119" :question/math-academy-id "q-2673" :question/problem "$(q+6)(q+2)=$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q+6)(q+2) &= q(q+2)+6(q+2) \\\\ &= {q}^{2}+2q+6q+12 \\\\ &= {q}^{2}+8q+12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58d24321-d687-5666-b444-c67333ddf888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2e-4f)(2e+4f)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485017} {:answer/id #uuid "883f814c-6c18-5941-967b-2ebea351df17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2e-4f)(2e-4f)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485113} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d7f83e-938d-58ce-b03e-2aa0fbe0f394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-2f)(e+2f)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485171} {:answer/id #uuid "b13e591a-bf5d-5ccb-bebd-477bcfb2fa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2e+4f)(2e+4f)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485202} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc225a0-8ce8-5951-977f-0f5267f0a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-2f)(e-2f)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d24321-d687-5666-b444-c67333ddf888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2e-4f)(2e+4f)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485017} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bdfff3f-3f1d-5467-b903-2b86371c55b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ca5943c-81c0-5244-b701-34175b2232ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-2833" :question/problem "Express $4{e}^{2}-16{f}^{2}$ as a product of two binomials." :question/worked-solution "We factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2}-16{f}^{2} &= (2e)^{2}-(4f)^{2} \\\\ &= (2e-4f)(2e+4f)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a73230d7-2608-5fda-9bd9-d380d1b68a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062474} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5c7bcc-d988-51a2-9faf-29f8684be33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062475} {:answer/id #uuid "51aa5b57-b075-512f-8ab1-4f61456e288d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062476} {:answer/id #uuid "54e07f76-634a-50b0-88d5-295949f8771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062477} {:answer/id #uuid "62156e2a-34ce-55e9-88d2-edcd5c90f59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e07f76-634a-50b0-88d5-295949f8771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062477} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a25c94-9e58-52b2-9281-07182838de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa8dfac6-2e07-5a12-91bc-c7ca479ccc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-31382" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ in the product $(x-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2}) &= x({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2})+(-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2}) \\\\ &= {x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}y-9x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}+9{y}^{3} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}y-15x{y}^{2}+9{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ is $5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34071898-2b9d-548e-be48-423897f0a5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062549} {:answer/id #uuid "2780d2db-b5d8-51a7-86fb-49f658cee304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544176} {:answer/id #uuid "730b8465-7159-5dad-8c03-b3cb57fc7371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544344} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5959f9-581b-5aad-9de2-fedc7cc48d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544399} {:answer/id #uuid "e65f7a1f-1218-5ac9-9a4e-4255893b3a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2780d2db-b5d8-51a7-86fb-49f658cee304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544176} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21dced6-1978-5aed-851e-0ce358a8924e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "842ce2e6-4b5f-56db-b8c0-d7317ee65b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-325" :question/problem "$\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}}=$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}} &= {z}^{(18-7)} \\\\ &= {z}^{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf05019e-0fbb-5909-bdc7-441afa5579c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062714} {:answer/id #uuid "d4630dd5-db80-5a2c-8abb-69a118e71dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062715} {:answer/id #uuid "e391f5b4-0444-5612-9d5d-cfb8b9723815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062716} {:answer/id #uuid "556d0f48-496d-5b83-a01c-ed647970c8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062717} {:answer/id #uuid "644225ae-897f-5530-9615-d488c54d900a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e391f5b4-0444-5612-9d5d-cfb8b9723815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062716} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0296223-0afa-55c4-985e-ee00bd370614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c3cdbb7-2f20-5471-9a71-577081e6a156" :question/math-academy-id "q-43479" :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{(\\frac{7}{3})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{(\\frac{7}{3})}=3÷\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $3=\\frac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $\\frac{7}{3}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{3}{7}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{7}{3} &= \\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 7} \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{(\\frac{7}{3})}=\\frac{9}{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109e6613-6e55-5b3c-b18a-2c3b10925fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480769} {:answer/id #uuid "88bfa841-9ff2-5a6a-8e9a-04cdcb7136de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481003} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3aa44a-8098-5049-9b4d-ccf6de1b1ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481096} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc92d42-9d4c-57e8-aebe-b1e85b358dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481128} {:answer/id #uuid "d657ff47-0060-560a-974e-93ba3c676167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88bfa841-9ff2-5a6a-8e9a-04cdcb7136de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481003} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a549313-5671-52e0-aede-0b763b3c9eef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b7d44bf-35f0-5ab1-9d61-a388510c7736" :question/math-academy-id "q-44735" :question/problem "$-{p}^{4}(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5})=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{p}^{4}(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}) &= (-{p}^{4})(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2})+(-{p}^{4})(3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}) \\\\ &= (\\,(-1)\\cdot (-7)\\,)({p}^{4}\\cdot {p}^{5})({q}^{2})+(\\,(-1)\\cdot 3)({p}^{4}\\cdot {p}^{2})({q}^{5}) \\\\ &= (7)({p}^{4+5})({q}^{2})+(-3)({p}^{4+2})({q}^{5}) \\\\ &= 7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d293c221-9b9a-5d3c-b74a-958f1204db3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063543} {:answer/id #uuid "67442e0d-bf9f-5d5a-a7b0-3e8f0d118c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485042} {:answer/id #uuid "79cb2700-bee0-5bf3-b755-57045a6c2ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{a}^{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485081} {:answer/id #uuid "abbe229a-cd02-5c11-ba12-ccd2a7a34207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485188} {:answer/id #uuid "b6957368-3b29-591b-8187-22d55305ec14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67442e0d-bf9f-5d5a-a7b0-3e8f0d118c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485042} :answer-field/id #uuid "8141b8b3-9a6c-5d0e-8486-e37d0221dabc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e67fcd0-74f3-542c-ab05-d6c58bbf219a" :question/math-academy-id "q-60572" :question/problem "$\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}}=$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}} &= {a}^{(5-9)} \\\\ &= {a}^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we convert the negative exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{-4} & =\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}}=\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd4b6b6-0e75-5946-aa7b-a2c81e452a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540663} {:answer/id #uuid "68de499e-e383-5af5-926d-9c7ff8a7a14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540706} {:answer/id #uuid "8a64610d-8f33-596a-ae2d-6e4a4d1ac047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540761} {:answer/id #uuid "93fcd4ad-0d95-5822-9f9a-c6e995d03ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540776} {:answer/id #uuid "eab6a494-8ff8-5e33-97a0-efc477f7317e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bd4b6b6-0e75-5946-aa7b-a2c81e452a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540663} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ae64f1-6b7b-57f5-a135-05c6e56fbe63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f4b0e01-d481-5aba-819f-1e32b934467a" :question/math-academy-id "q-60574" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{{x}^{-5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{{x}^{-5}} &= {x}^{-2-(-5)} \\\\ &= {x}^{-2+5} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068432 :question/id #uuid "a474788c-5b2b-570d-84bf-2c9575ac4f54" :question/math-academy-id "e-10022" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{8z^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $8z^3$ as the product $4z^2 \\cdot 2z$ which contains a perfect square $(4z^2){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8z^3} &= \\sqrt{4z^2 \\cdot 2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4z^2 \\cdot 2z} &= \\sqrt{(2z)^2\\cdot 2z} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{(2z)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(2z)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2z} &= |2z|\\cdot \\sqrt{2z} \\\\[3pt] &= |2|\\cdot|z|\\cdot \\sqrt{2z} \\\\[3pt] &= 2|z|\\sqrt{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068442 :question/id #uuid "dd7c79e5-d274-5f04-af44-335b5a6d95df" :question/math-academy-id "e-10699" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4z^2}{9}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac {4z^2} {9}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{4z^2}} {\\sqrt{9}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{\\sqrt{3^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}}}{3} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}}}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{|2z|}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{|2|\\cdot |z|}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot |z|}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{2|z|}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068443 :question/id #uuid "40af3166-710b-508c-9a8e-2c54365cd238" :question/math-academy-id "e-10712" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}}$ given that $x > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}} = \\sqrt {\\dfrac {32x^5} {2x^3}} = \\sqrt {16x^2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt {16x^2} &= \\sqrt {4^2x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt {(4x)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= |4x| \\\\[5pt] &= |4| \\cdot |x| \\\\[5pt] &= 4 \\cdot |x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 \\cdot |x| &= 4 \\cdot x \\\\[5pt] &= 4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068444 :question/id #uuid "9ceac090-9a1c-57d7-a311-f771ece6e0b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10716" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac {45a^4} {5} }= \\sqrt{9a^4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{a}^{4}} &= \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ &= |3{a}^{2}| \\\\ &= |3|\\cdot |{a}^{2}| \\\\ &= 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}| &= 3\\cdot {a}^{2} \\\\ &= 3{a}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068460 :question/id #uuid "4ad21d3a-bf40-5b65-8ba6-320d853e6cff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12869" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-1, 4)$ and has a slope of $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line passes through the point $(-1,4)$ and its slope is $2$, we substitute $x=-1$, $y=4,$ and $m=2$ into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty &= mx + b \\\\\n\t(4) &= (2)(-1) + b \\\\\n\t4 &= -2 + b \\\\\n\t6 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $b=6.$ The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068465 :question/id #uuid "e9c8bdc9-696d-5a55-bb0d-d247008c5684" :question/math-academy-id "e-1333" :question/problem "Factor the expression $7a^2+28a+28.$" :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $7.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}^{2}+28a+28 &= 7({a}^{2}+4a+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+4a+4 &= {a}^{2}+4a+{2}^{2} \\\\ &= {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 2a+{2}^{2} \\\\ &= (a+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({a}^{2}+4a+4) &= 7(a+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068467 :question/id #uuid "2ca53a08-7b11-552c-983f-96e7a392156c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1357" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-8x^6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-8{x}^{6}} &= \\sqrt[3]{(-2{x}^{2})\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{(-2{x}^{2})^{3}} \\\\ &= -2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068468 :question/id #uuid "c52719a7-544b-5844-b716-9b9d2999c028" :question/math-academy-id "e-1358" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{a^3}{(b-1)^3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{a}^{3}}{(b-1)^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{a}{b-1})}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{a}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068469 :question/id #uuid "969be012-a830-564e-a355-00a4f1985ae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1363" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{18y^3}$ given that $y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $18y^3$ as the product $9y^2\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(9y^2).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{18y^3} &= \\sqrt{9y^2 \\cdot 2y} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{9y^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(3y)^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= |3y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= |3| \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y \\geq 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= 3 \\cdot y \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 3y\\sqrt{2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068472 :question/id #uuid "0901e241-519b-50ee-ab5f-50b2f631e948" :question/math-academy-id "e-13932" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $0 \\leq 3x+6 < 15.$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t0 &\\leq 3x+6 < 15 \\\\[5pt]\n\t0 - 6 &\\leq 3x + 6 -6 < 15-6 \\\\[5pt]\n\t-6 &\\leq 3x < 9 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\dfrac{-6}{3} &\\leq \\dfrac{3x}{3} < \\dfrac{9}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\t-2 &\\leq x < 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-2 \\leq x < 3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068477 :question/id #uuid "9c00e3e1-9337-5380-af51-e7e8c7ae9f48" :question/math-academy-id "e-13970" :question/problem "Find $t$ if $2t^2 = 36.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the $t^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t2t^2 &= 36\\\\\n\t\tt^2 &= 18\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\t\t\n\t\t\\sqrt{t^2} &= \\sqrt{18} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= \\sqrt{18} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\t\t\\vert t \\vert &= 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\t\tt &= \\pm 3\\sqrt{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068486 :question/id #uuid "38228952-21ef-5caf-9805-76e42ebf0caa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14148" :question/problem "If $g(x) = (x-4)^2+3(5-x)$, calculate $g(2).$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tg(x) &= (x-4)^2+3(5-x) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\tg(2) &= (2-4)^2+3(5-2) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t      &= (-2)^2+3(3) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t      &= 4 + 9 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t      &= 13\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068512 :question/id #uuid "8e7b0730-4273-5eef-a9a3-4a9e91c62ff4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14829" :question/problem "Solve $3p^2 = 5p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t3p^2 &= 5p \\\\[3pt]\n\t3p^2 - 5p &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\tp(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\np=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 3p-5=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\np=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad p=\\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=0,\\dfrac{5}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068535 :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} \n&= \\lim_{x\\to3}  \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {3x^2}{1}\\\\[5pt]\n&=  3(3)^2\\\\[5pt]\n&= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068536 :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ But that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'}\\\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1}\\\\[5pt]\n&= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068537 :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} .$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068538 :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068559 :question/id #uuid "d659780a-f726-5827-a13a-a64b21a0cf41" :question/math-academy-id "e-4027" :question/problem "What are the solutions to $x^2 + 16 = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx^2 + 16 &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-16}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = \\pm 4 \\textrm{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068560 :question/id #uuid "8082e3bd-04dc-5598-93c5-dbc423cdba97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4028" :question/problem "If $p^2 + 27 = 0,$ then $p =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^2 + 27 & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n\np^2 & = -27 \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\sqrt{-27}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\\pm \\sqrt{-27} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{27\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{27}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}\\cdot 3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p = \\pm 3\\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068561 :question/id #uuid "99f522f8-39ff-5612-86d1-84e703bde35f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4029" :question/problem "If $16z^2 + 49 = 0,$ then $z =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16z^2 + 49 & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n\n16z^2 & = -49 \\\\[5pt]\n\nz^2 & = -\\dfrac{49}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{49}{16}}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{49}}{\\sqrt{16}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=\\pm \\dfrac{7}{4} \\textrm{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068562 :question/id #uuid "93aec687-49af-55ea-9212-b33dd29f4ce7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4030" :question/problem "What are the solutions of the equation $2y^2 + 9 = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t2y^2 + 9 &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\n\t\t2y^2 &= -9\\\\[5pt]\n\ny^2 & = -\\dfrac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{9}{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\textrm{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068577 :question/id #uuid "eb4964f1-0462-5a41-b712-3d0d4dbd8c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4967" :question/problem "Factor $x^3 - 8.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the given expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 8 = x^3 - 2^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^3 - 8 & = x^3 - 2^3\\\\[3pt]\n\n& = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2\\cdot x + 2^2\\right)\\\\[3pt]\n\n& = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2x + 4\\right)\t\t\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068582 :question/id #uuid "4968972d-3bc9-5100-aa70-46213e0c3581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5028" :question/problem "Is $x=3$ a root of the polynomial $2x^3+8x^2-2x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)^3+8(3)^2-2(3)-8 &= 2(27)+8(9)-6-8 \\\\ &= 54+72-14 \\\\ &= 112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$\n\nTherefore, $3$ is *not* a root."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068590 :question/id #uuid "bea63d2c-7283-5b7d-997a-8f6f583fd0cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5126" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{2+a}{2a} + \\dfrac{3+2a}{2a}$." :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $2a$, so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 + a}{2a} + \\frac{3 + 2a}{2a} &= \\frac{2 + a + 3 + 2a}{2a} \\\\ &= \\frac{2 + 3 + a + 2a}{2a} \\\\ &= \\frac{5 + 3a}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068592 :question/id #uuid "1da3fbaf-ef04-56b7-8ddc-338e797b00db" :question/math-academy-id "e-5129" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{y}{x(1+y)} + \\dfrac{y}{1+y}$." :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $x(y+1)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y)/(x(1 + y)) + \\frac{y}{1 + y} &= (y)/(x(1 + y)) + \\frac{x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{y}{1 + y} \\\\ &= (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (xy)/(x(1 + y)) \\\\ &= (y + xy)/(x(1 + y)) \\\\ &= (y(1 + x))/(x(1 + y))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068593 :question/id #uuid "5d90ba01-2318-5f03-9d7a-a7ad66555886" :question/math-academy-id "e-5131" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} .$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} \n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 3} {x \\cdot y^2}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {6} {xy^2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068596 :question/id #uuid "e521a677-4e95-5c86-80c1-447924b6d34d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5134" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \n\t\t&=\\dfrac {3(p - 4)} {(p + 3)(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {3\\cancel{(p - 4)}} {\\cancel{(p + 3)}(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\cancel{p + 3}} {\\cancel{p - 4}} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {3}{p - 3}\n\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068597 :question/id #uuid "4fba84c9-6c0f-51cd-b5f2-8b469bd8346f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5135" :question/problem "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac y {6x^2}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac y {6x^2} &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^2} {xy} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x} {y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068598 :question/id #uuid "fa6bbedb-654a-58a5-8fcf-3bb0fd038160" :question/math-academy-id "e-5136" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $x \\div y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\div  \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068604 :question/id #uuid "dd8b5378-4da1-5955-a566-f84ef0cecb28" :question/math-academy-id "e-5206" :question/problem "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac {5}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac 5 y &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5 \\\\ &= \\dfrac {3y} {5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068605 :question/id #uuid "3fd58494-e5ee-5dc7-af3c-959888f59627" :question/math-academy-id "e-5207" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 \\\\ &= 2a^2b - 10b\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068647 :question/id #uuid "8168f846-013a-529a-a916-501438b2ca30" :question/math-academy-id "e-7316" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)}\\,\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $A.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)(x^2 + 1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6=A({x}^{2}+1)+{x}^{2}-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}-6 & =A({0}^{2}+1)+{0}^{2}-9 \\\\ -6 & =A-9 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{4x^2 - 6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{3}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1}\\right) \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = 3\\int\\dfrac{1}{x-3}\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm d x + 3\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x-3} \\textrm{d}x = \\ln|x-3|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $x^2 + 1 = u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{u}\\right) \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{2} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert u\\vert \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert x^2 + 1\\vert \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\left(x^2+1\\right).\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\arctan{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-6}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068653 :question/id #uuid "3df1f00a-3a87-5db7-83c5-d47dce633bf9" :question/math-academy-id "e-843" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x} &= \\dfrac {6y + 2} {x(3y + 1)} \\\\[5pt] \n        &=\\dfrac {2(3y + 1)} {x(3y + 1)} \\\\[5pt] \n        &=\\dfrac {2\\cancel{(3y + 1)} } {x \\cancel{(3y + 1)} } \\\\[5pt] \n        &=\\dfrac {2 } {x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068670 :question/id #uuid "d086fc71-f668-5ce7-8fd0-b046a3c228eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-989" :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line that passes through the points $(1, 5)$ and $(4, 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We let $(x_1,y_1) = (1,5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tm &= \\dfrac {y_2 - y_1} {x_2 - x_1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {2 - 5} {4 - 1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {-3}{3} \\\\\n\t\t    &= -1\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068676 :question/id #uuid "ce2e3248-96e3-5d69-9a16-1a62e0ce0eeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-9980" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{50u^7}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $50u^7$ as the product $25u^6\\cdot 2u$ which contains a perfect square $(25u^6){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{50u^7} &= \\sqrt{25u^6\\cdot 2u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25u^6\\cdot 2u} &= \\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2\\cdot 2u} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2u} &= |5u^3|\\cdot\\sqrt{2u} \\\\[3pt] &= |5|\\cdot |u^3|\\cdot\\sqrt{2u} \\\\[3pt] &= 5|u|^3 \\sqrt{2u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12aaa2df-cbfc-5d4a-8f1a-02ca44658d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25({y}^{2}+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483599} {:answer/id #uuid "318d748e-c355-5a16-a7b0-f432ca157de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(5y+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483653} {:answer/id #uuid "55a4d5a2-d6d5-5c01-98a8-f81176186f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(5{y}^{2}+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483722} {:answer/id #uuid "94ba7ec0-9791-572d-ba76-db6608a2e991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(5y+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483874} {:answer/id #uuid "ca444773-3498-5cfe-aabc-eb03a3f76fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca444773-3498-5cfe-aabc-eb03a3f76fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483970} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d801e5-57bf-5096-8355-daa85cc37fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11047f98-24ac-5e85-994b-d2cd73e1984b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3009" :question/problem "Factor $25{y}^{2}+50y+25.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $25.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $25,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}+50y+25 &= 25({y}^{2}+2y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y+1 &= {y}^{2}+2y+{1}^{2} \\\\ &= {y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2} \\\\ &= (y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({y}^{2}+2y+1) &= 25(y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9604b624-c03b-5347-ba7b-8d49db33c3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\sqrt{8y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066523} {:answer/id #uuid "e9919e2c-cbe1-5c41-a1b1-57be787bd108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y\\sqrt{4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066525} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab5aca2-34cf-511d-b92f-1e7702541d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y\\sqrt{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066526} {:answer/id #uuid "64285070-c3b5-5963-aa25-3bf6449987b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8y\\sqrt{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066527} {:answer/id #uuid "effe64da-d5ba-51cb-959c-be6a879f598d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{8y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ab5aca2-34cf-511d-b92f-1e7702541d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y\\sqrt{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066526} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9291b82-8dbe-52ac-a018-87c4d7471cd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09ae7f43-8e12-5216-a76e-64db91dde2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-3052" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{32{y}^{3}}$ given that $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $32{y}^{3}$ as the product $16{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(16{y}^{2}).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{32{y}^{3}} &= \\sqrt{16{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}\\cdot 2y} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= |4y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= |4|\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 4\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= 4\\cdot y\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 4y\\sqrt{2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c74308bb-5312-5958-b720-065684db080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066553} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} {:answer/id #uuid "28a7f235-57a0-53cc-a737-590d73e17205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066556} {:answer/id #uuid "23dbda11-5ccf-5052-90ff-4a04744a31b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503050} {:answer/id #uuid "d40037db-e219-58c5-9dc7-f0a266a679e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} :answer-field/id #uuid "46437d48-53c7-532c-8f90-902783817395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae074857-e82d-57ff-b966-0c2516135214" :question/math-academy-id "q-3094" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}$ given that $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}} &= \\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{5})}^{2}} \\\\ &= |\\frac{2x}{5}| \\\\ &= \\frac{|2x|}{|5|} \\\\ &= \\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5} &= \\frac{2\\cdot x}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3dae21e-7c38-5471-8c2d-20bd496b5a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{b}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066833} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd25107-9cf9-5a36-a04d-2ad782f868d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4b}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066835} {:answer/id #uuid "4193aae6-2008-57e2-9909-1647fdce4c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4b}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066836} {:answer/id #uuid "544dc548-d21b-5d8c-9098-7b6f1b580f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066837} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfe5412-1f1d-58e4-94f8-bff7c885cdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{b}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4193aae6-2008-57e2-9909-1647fdce4c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4b}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066836} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5e56cd-146b-5ba8-a44b-1c881fd8150a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4cb8a33-f9e8-5bcd-ae95-f5d61bf439b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3497" :question/problem "$\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20} &= \\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{20}{36b} \\\\ &= \\frac{12b\\cdot b}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 4}{12b\\cdot 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{12b}\\cdot b}{\\cancel{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4}{\\cancel{12b}\\cdot 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4b}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7ff7de-90de-558c-8f2d-528864d245da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{a}^{2}}{7bc}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319535} {:answer/id #uuid "39a65ca4-e4ef-5b7e-ae77-cc4a377e3bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{c}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319569} {:answer/id #uuid "443246d4-fe48-5008-bfd4-3475729ce5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7bc}{4{a}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319594} {:answer/id #uuid "738585b6-ddd2-52ce-86fa-42ef30add9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7bc}{4{a}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319737} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9b211b-88cb-522e-ac44-6cc58c295fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7bc}{4{a}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738585b6-ddd2-52ce-86fa-42ef30add9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7bc}{4{a}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319737} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6f2afa2-cdb8-5f64-8c45-d48f6a0d96fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "452b3a54-8a97-5cfc-8253-05f85217902c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3526" :question/problem "What is $\\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}÷\\frac{8{a}^{3}}{7{c}^{4}}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}÷\\frac{8{a}^{3}}{7{c}^{4}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}÷\\frac{8{a}^{3}}{7{c}^{4}} &= \\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{7{c}^{4}}{8{a}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{2ab\\cdot b}{b{c}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{7c\\cdot {c}^{3}}{4\\cdot 2a\\cdot {a}^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{2a}b\\cdot \\cancel{b}}{\\cancel{b}\\cancel{{c}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{7c\\cdot \\cancel{{c}^{3}}}{4\\cdot \\cancel{2a}\\cdot {a}^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{7bc}{4{a}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b8fb14-c0e0-59ff-9dcc-0cfee40893f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067067} {:answer/id #uuid "0501fd5c-c073-5ad6-b7e1-e58ff883086a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067068} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9c9a20-208b-5bf1-89b3-8f27461cb543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067069} {:answer/id #uuid "58197747-933e-54cf-ae88-668f447c54a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067070} {:answer/id #uuid "7274c8da-3be2-5d2d-aed6-509173699a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58197747-933e-54cf-ae88-668f447c54a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067070} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fd6c60-d87e-5fe2-a103-1257520e8a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b7c4071-ca3e-544d-9aa0-e1f8f9fa4fb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3895" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{4}-13{x}^{2}+36?$\n\nI. $x=4$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=-4$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{4}-13(4)^{2}+36 &= 256-13(16)+36 \\\\ &= 256-208+36 \\\\ &= 84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-13(2)^{2}+36 &= 16-13(4)+36 \\\\ &= 16-52+36 \\\\ &= 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $-4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-13(-4)^{2}+36 &= 256-13(16)+36 \\\\ &= 256-208+36 \\\\ &= 84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=-4,$ so the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15017b89-6a83-59c3-8ca4-1e65d3e32c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{1-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324161} {:answer/id #uuid "3a903b7b-dcca-5c4f-8920-9a1546e6dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324443} {:answer/id #uuid "c79526ea-db45-5cfa-a2b6-882255022a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14y}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325697} {:answer/id #uuid "dc042986-d3e3-5167-8273-d4f156249300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26y-45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325890} {:answer/id #uuid "e379b819-7fe0-521e-bbc1-1371d8e50ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+9}{1-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a903b7b-dcca-5c4f-8920-9a1546e6dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324443} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e51594-6076-558b-b4c1-486d8c2bf0d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e010d20d-afd2-59fa-8a1c-50d451ce4776" :question/math-academy-id "q-41576" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}=p$ and $\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}=q,$ what is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to add the following fractions:\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5(1-2y)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} &= \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} \\\\ &= \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} \\\\ &= \\frac{6y+5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} \\\\ &= \\frac{6y+45-20y}{5(1-2y)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47261d33-2213-50a0-9864-077f33793a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324546} {:answer/id #uuid "5db8f67b-cd93-58f5-a9c3-bc5afd63c312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11-2b}{3b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324750} {:answer/id #uuid "85944ca6-5bcd-5de3-8400-88e8638b9a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2b+1}{3b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325100} {:answer/id #uuid "b2786940-6b21-54ef-a63d-568f58537c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2b-1}{3b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325501} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c5201a-6f92-5fff-84e0-f1c19a018ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11+2b}{3b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47261d33-2213-50a0-9864-077f33793a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324546} :answer-field/id #uuid "0350aa13-d724-54d9-adf4-a0e03f6fa921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f24c09b5-350f-5383-a4ad-ef306d26e28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-41579" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{5+b}{3b}+\\frac{6-b}{3b}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $3b,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5+b}{3b}+\\frac{6-b}{3b} &= \\frac{5+b+6-b}{3b} \\\\ &= \\frac{(5+6)+(b-b)}{3b} \\\\ &= \\frac{11}{3b}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db7862b-5783-5ab9-a17c-fc752181c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067715} {:answer/id #uuid "afd89451-dbcc-525a-914e-caa711a2abb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067716} {:answer/id #uuid "ea601f60-3ff6-518d-b00e-24fb09177034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067718} {:answer/id #uuid "6c911733-8c6b-5b54-b28b-ffba8ec043c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067719} {:answer/id #uuid "b1595b53-a21f-533f-948c-76530eaa78e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{b}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db7862b-5783-5ab9-a17c-fc752181c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067715} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a19cef-79e8-55b0-b52d-817fbf5bad48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c88910e9-93dc-5fa8-9def-79b320b71eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-52492" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{4}{b})}^{3}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{4}{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bca481-7d34-5259-91cb-874aca82b8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498885} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0d4325-0f8d-5187-a9a0-6346e4c4c09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498914} {:answer/id #uuid "6831cc29-926d-5a0f-9b44-864c4b8510e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(1-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498964} {:answer/id #uuid "75522216-0f2f-585a-a400-b83699659bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498991} {:answer/id #uuid "8516910a-3ad3-5900-bf26-9abcee760676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8516910a-3ad3-5900-bf26-9abcee760676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499029} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8712f6-aae6-50a7-bb10-c5dc0c11f25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49c6fb96-74c6-5438-982d-666dbe67b2e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-52493" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{27(x-1)^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27(x-1)^{3}} &= \\sqrt[3]{3(x-1)\\cdot 3(x-1)\\cdot 3(x-1)} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(3(x-1))}^{3}} \\\\ &= 3(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bbde06-85b1-5835-9118-e127a4019b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7b}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333330} {:answer/id #uuid "42fbbef0-4b70-5b42-8357-7aecc460bb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333613} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb39d13-026c-54ab-86bc-606fda5a4191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333683} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b44540-ed26-5dc1-8ff7-01bb46c12f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5b}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334852} {:answer/id #uuid "e320734a-e597-5264-b68c-21d22b8f7138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35{b}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fbbef0-4b70-5b42-8357-7aecc460bb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333613} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f3ec3c-3fa1-5f9e-863c-f9a4e8ad93d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17f8926a-edda-5159-87e5-d53ce755ff9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-69924" :question/problem "$\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7} &= \\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{b} \\\\ &= \\frac{35{b}^{2}}{2b} \\\\ &= \\frac{35{b}^{\\cancel{2}}}{2\\cancel{b}} \\\\ &= \\frac{35b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $b$ in the numerator and denominator."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50dfdf7-8857-5c81-86e1-ab156863e446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068177} {:answer/id #uuid "46b2ef80-9a66-596b-b82c-62ba6d9db3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068178} {:answer/id #uuid "699507e5-dbe9-500d-b628-50a2c020569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068180} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2218cc-e7d1-5950-a8c1-23a19628e3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068181} {:answer/id #uuid "504e0f2a-072f-5b1b-b073-c5b324045add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x{z}^{2}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50dfdf7-8857-5c81-86e1-ab156863e446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068177} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de203fc-783b-5935-9ac5-c4ced5ae123a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dfcab98-cba3-546d-aba3-178a79398a49" :question/math-academy-id "q-69925" :question/problem "$\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5} &= \\frac{3xz}{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2z} \\\\ &= \\frac{15xz}{8z} \\\\ &= \\frac{15x\\cancel{z}}{8\\cancel{z}} \\\\ &= \\frac{15x}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $z$ in the numerator and denominator."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af2c7545-0451-5650-a5ee-5ce78934bb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5b}{14a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068189} {:answer/id #uuid "b08d49d1-afb8-5b7f-8e54-52a31f7bf52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5ab}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068190} {:answer/id #uuid "21ccfac9-6446-510b-9480-3adfd50163e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5ab}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068191} {:answer/id #uuid "256e53f7-83ac-59c1-b6b5-154da604264e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2ab}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068192} {:answer/id #uuid "43671ebb-f87e-5d7c-aae5-72d6ceb5c415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5a}{14b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ccfac9-6446-510b-9480-3adfd50163e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5ab}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068191} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f68fc8f-c074-5982-9412-9c3d56d22f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f813024f-885c-5619-a6ed-c7af16a7f48d" :question/math-academy-id "q-69965" :question/problem "$\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a} &= \\frac{5b}{7}\\cdot \\frac{a}{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{5ab}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073464 :question/id #uuid "cf793c45-6c89-50c8-bb28-d066d2b8342a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1370" :question/problem "Simplify $8\\sqrt 3 - 5\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5.$" :question/worked-solution "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5} &= (8-5)\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\ &= 3\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073476 :question/id #uuid "145782cb-fa17-5870-a174-16552a6b236b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13890" :question/problem "By rounding the dividend to the nearest whole number, estimate the value of $94.8 \\div 5$." :question/worked-solution "First, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n$94.8 \\rightarrow 95$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $95 \\div 5$. Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n| $1$$9$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n95 \\div 5 = 19\n$$\n\nTherefore, $94.8 \\div 5$ is approximately equal to $19$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073478 :question/id #uuid "7272264d-b07f-58d7-b084-24ede4cc2243" :question/math-academy-id "e-13896" :question/problem "Estimate the value of $206.6 \\div 3.43$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number." :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$206.6\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 207 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}3$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\div 3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\div 3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073479 :question/id #uuid "b223571e-3070-5474-a01c-f909c89bf36d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13900" :question/problem "Estimate the value of $116.8 \\div 5.15$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number. Round your final answer to the nearest whole number." :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$116.8\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 117 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}5$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\div 5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\div 5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073524 :question/id #uuid "83452ea8-bf0c-54eb-8ed5-54bb3c200d2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1812" :question/problem "Harold cuts an $85$-inch long rope into $5$ pieces of the same size. How long is each piece?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how long each of the pieces is, we need to divide $85$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 5 = 3$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}7$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}35} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}7}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 35$Subtract: ${\\color{red}35} - 35 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $85$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n85 \\div 5 = 17\n$$\n\nThis means that each piece of rope is $17$ inches long."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073525 :question/id #uuid "00006ac6-b956-55b9-9bac-e83cdee6e998" :question/math-academy-id "e-1813" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing digits in the following division problem?\n\n| $1$$2$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$7$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$1$ |\n| --- |" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $7 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 7$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 7 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$\\fbox{1}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}15} \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $7 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 14$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 14 = \\fbox{1}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $85$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $1$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n85 \\div 7 = 12\\,\\text{R}\\,1\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are $1$, $7$ and $1$:\n\n| $\\fbox{1}$$2$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$\\fbox{7}$$\\fbox{1}$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$1$ |\n| --- |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073526 :question/id #uuid "c4ce9599-6ea0-5f35-ba9a-eb7f1ad42ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1814" :question/problem "What is $86$ divided by $2$?" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}4$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $4$$\\color{blue}3$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $86$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n86 \\div 2 = 43\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073527 :question/id #uuid "231a328c-fcbb-56c0-b0f7-40dc68bc34bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-1815" :question/problem "Find the quotient and the remainder of $89\\div 6$." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$2$$\\color{lightgray}9$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,2$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 6 = 2$Bring down: $9$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}9$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$\\fbox{5}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}29} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}4}\\,\\text{R}\\,5$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 24$Subtract: ${\\color{red}29} - 24 = \\fbox{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $89$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $5$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n89 \\div 6 = 14\\,\\text{R}\\,5\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073528 :question/id #uuid "b83956c9-d606-5ce0-bc37-c00a9498e61f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1819" :question/problem "What is $847 \\div 6$?" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$2$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,2$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 6 = 2$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}24} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 24$Subtract: ${\\color{red}24} - 24 = 0$Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$2$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$0$$\\color{red}7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{1}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}7} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}7} - 6 = \\fbox{1}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $847$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $1$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n847 \\div 6 = 141\\,\\text{R}\\,1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073529 :question/id #uuid "99d7e5f9-0989-54d9-aa02-4a4a97365176" :question/math-academy-id "e-1820" :question/problem "Find the **quotient** in the following long division problem:\n$6 \\: \\enclose{longdiv}{3 \\: 7\\: 9}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of hundreds in ${\\color{red}3}79$) is less than the divisor $6$. Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}6$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}9$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}37} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}6}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 36$Subtract: ${\\color{red}37} - 36 = 1$Bring down: $9$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $6$$\\color{blue}3$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}9$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$\\fbox{1}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}19} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 18$Subtract: ${\\color{red}19} - 18 = 1$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $379$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $1$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n379 \\div 6 = 63\\,\\text{R}\\,1\n$$\n\nSo, the quotient is $63$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073530 :question/id #uuid "0a15fdeb-2ae0-51d2-b35e-0e1dadde8de4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1821" :question/problem "What is $608 \\div 3$?" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}0$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $3$ but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used. Therefore, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $2$$0$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $2$$0$$\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{2}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,2$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 6 = \\fbox{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $608$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $2$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n608 \\div 3 = 202\\,\\text{R}\\,2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073531 :question/id #uuid "cd27e652-7f49-518f-b46d-8d8fec4605dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-1822" :question/problem "A rocket, flying at a constant speed, covered a distance of $2,088$ miles in a period of $6$ minutes. How many miles did the rocket cover in $1$ minute?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how many miles the rocket covered in $1$ minute, we need to divide $2,088$ by $6$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}2$ (the number of thousands in ${\\color{red}2},088$) is less than the divisor $6$. Therefore, we should consider both the thousands and hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}2} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$2$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}20} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\text{R}\\,2$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 18$Subtract: ${\\color{red}20} - 18 = 2$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}4$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$4$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}28} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}4}\\,\\text{R}\\,4$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 24$Subtract: ${\\color{red}28} - 24 = 4$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $3$$4$$\\color{blue}8$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}48} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}8}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}8$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}8} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}48} - 48 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $2,088$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2,088 \\div 6 = 348\n$$\n\nSo, the rocket covered $348$ miles every minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073532 :question/id #uuid "73d06a12-f579-5f93-97ac-669330a06236" :question/math-academy-id "e-1823" :question/problem "What are the quotient and the remainder of dividing $3,634$ by $4$?" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of thousands in ${\\color{red}3},634$) is less than the divisor $4$. Therefore, we should consider both the thousands and hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}9$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}36} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}9}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}9$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}9} = 36$Subtract: ${\\color{red}36} - 36 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $9$$\\color{blue}0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$\\color{red}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}3} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}0}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}3$ is less than the divisor $4$ but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used. Therefore, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $9$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$3$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $9$$0$$\\color{blue}8$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$\\fbox{2}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}34} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}8}\\,\\text{R}\\,\\,2$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}8$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}8} = 32$Subtract: ${\\color{red}34} - 32 = \\fbox{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $3,634$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $2$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore, when we divide $3,634$ by $4$,\n\n- the quotient is $908$, and\n- the remainder is $2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073533 :question/id #uuid "ffb56b62-cec0-5d5d-ab88-3b30779aa8e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1824" :question/problem "Find the value of $6,096 \\div 6$." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $6$ but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used. Therefore, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}9$ | Bring down: $9$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{red}9$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 6 = \\fbox{3}$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$1$$\\color{blue}6$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}36} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}6}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 36$Subtract: ${\\color{red}36} - 36 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $6,096$ so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6,096 \\div 6 = 1,016\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073534 :question/id #uuid "9e662552-a456-50fa-bdc4-1206ca713791" :question/math-academy-id "e-1825" :question/problem "Find the quotient and the remainder of $8,077$ divided by $8$." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $8$ but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used. Therefore, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}0$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$0$$\\color{red}7$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}7} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}0}\\,\\text{R}\\,7$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}7$ is less than the divisor $8$ but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used. Therefore, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$0$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$7$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider both tens and ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$0$$\\color{blue}9$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$0$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\fbox{5}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}77} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}9}\\,\\text{R}\\,5$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}9$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}9} = 72$Subtract: ${\\color{red}77} - 72 = \\fbox{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $8,077$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $5$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8,077 \\div 8 = 1,009\\,\\text{R}\\,5\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073545 :question/id #uuid "3c5626bf-2c51-5154-a1e9-63e7325a9f8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2039" :question/problem "What is $7.2$ divided by $0.6$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2}{0.6}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2\\times 10}{0.6\\times 10} = \\dfrac{72}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $72\\div 6$. We will use long division:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}7} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}7} - 6 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n72 \\div 6 = 12\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 0.6 = 12 \\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073546 :question/id #uuid "15aeeda1-5a03-5476-84d5-88c2971ad8f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-2040" :question/problem "What is $86.5 \\div 0.5$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{865}{5}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $865\\div 5$. We will use long division:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nCarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$7$$3$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$${0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n865 \\div 5 = 173\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n86.5 \\div 0.5 = 173 \\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073547 :question/id #uuid "5145d781-15c7-5bc9-8389-19334f821d94" :question/math-academy-id "e-2042" :question/problem "What is $9.45$ divided by $0.09$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45}{0.09}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Here, there are $2$ decimal places, so we need to multiply by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45\\times 100}{0.09\\times 100} = \\dfrac{945}{9}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $945\\div 9$. We will use long division:\n\n| $9$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nApplying the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$9$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe found that\n\n$$\n945 \\div 9 = 105\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n9.45 \\div 0.09 = 105 \\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073548 :question/id #uuid "8a6dbba4-96c3-5f39-b0d3-abc9cfeab392" :question/math-academy-id "e-2047" :question/problem "Abby wants to cut a $63$ inch piece of wire into four smaller pieces of equal size. How long should each piece be? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "To determine the length of each piece, we need to divide $63$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}5} \\:$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe went through all the digits and reached a remainder of $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n63 \\div 4= 15.75\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $5$, which is greater than or equal to $5$, so we round up:\n\n$$\n15.75 \\rightarrow 15.8\n$$\n\nWe conclude that each piece would be about $15.8$ inches long."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073549 :question/id #uuid "e6332afa-2566-5a36-89d1-ae127b6c662e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2048" :question/problem "Round the value of $137 \\div 8$ to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}2} \\: \\ldots$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$5$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place, we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n137 \\div 8 = 17.12\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $2$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$17.12\\ldots \\rightarrow 17.1\\,$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073550 :question/id #uuid "3a834cf6-d46e-551e-a1c3-7649e781d088" :question/math-academy-id "e-2049" :question/problem "Round the value of $66 \\div 9$ to the nearest hundredth." :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}3}\\: \\ldots$$9$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $2$ decimal places (the nearest hundredth), we stop after finding the quotient to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n66 \\div 9 = 7.333\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest hundredth. The thousandths place is $3$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$7.333\\ldots \\rightarrow 7.33\\,$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073551 :question/id #uuid "96972378-c7c1-55e3-b333-eeeabb10e5ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-2051" :question/problem "What is $54.8 \\div 4$?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n54.8 \\div 4 = 13.7 \\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073552 :question/id #uuid "a622f6ba-cf23-59ee-951b-1c21654983a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2052" :question/problem "To make $4$ servings of ice cream, Rita used $3.4$ ounces of cocoa powder. How much cocoa powder did she use for each serving?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount of cocoa powder Rita used to make each serving, we need to divide $3.4$ by $4$. To do this, we can use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\; \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\, 0 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}5$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\, 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}0}$$\\,-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.4 \\div 4 = 0.85 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Rita used $0.85$ ounces of cocoa powder to make each serving of ice cream."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073553 :question/id #uuid "8060f93b-78f8-5d31-ad4d-6c44674961b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2054" :question/problem "$0.0694 \\div 0.2=$" :question/worked-solution "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694}{0.2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694 \\times 10}{0.2\\times 10} = \\dfrac{0.694}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $0.694 \\div 2$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $0 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,. \\,} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n0.694 \\div 2 = 0.347\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.0694 \\div 0.2 = 0.347 \\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073554 :question/id #uuid "36b548ff-acf9-52e4-a253-db809625bdae" :question/math-academy-id "e-2055" :question/problem "What is $0.471 \\div 0.02$?" :question/worked-solution "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471}{0.02}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471 \\times 100}{0.02\\times 100} = \\dfrac{47.1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $47.1 \\div 2$. We can compute this using the standard algorithm:\n\n| $\\,2\\,\\,$$\\,3\\,$.$\\,5\\,\\,$$\\,5\\,\\,$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}\\,} \\!\\!\\!.\\, \\!1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n47.1 \\div 2 = 23.55\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.471 \\div 0.02 = 23.55 \\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073657 :question/id #uuid "4e4c3088-724e-56c3-bb7b-14df1eb1327c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8107" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing digits in the following long division problem?\n\n| $1$$1$$5$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$3$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$2$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,2$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 4 = 2$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$1$$\\color{blue}5$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{3}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}23} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}5}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}5$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 20$Subtract: ${\\color{red}23} - 20 = \\fbox{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $463$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $3$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n463 \\div 4 = 115\\,\\text{R}\\,3\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are $1,6$ and $3$.\n\n| $1$$\\fbox{1}$$5$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073663 :question/id #uuid "e5202e06-b6a8-5d74-a395-fe7c56b8e9bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-9728" :question/problem "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5} +5 \\sqrt{10} -3 \\sqrt[3]{40}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{40} & =\\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5}=2\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-3\\sqrt[3]{40} &= 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-3(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) \\\\ &= 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-6\\sqrt[3]{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-6\\sqrt[3]{5} &= 5\\sqrt{10}+(2\\sqrt[3]{5}-6\\sqrt[3]{5}) \\\\ &= 5\\sqrt{10}+(2-6)\\sqrt[3]{5} \\\\ &= 5\\sqrt{10}-4\\sqrt[3]{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073664 :question/id #uuid "c1f1d037-45a6-5e72-a735-0c0512290120" :question/math-academy-id "e-9729" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $3\\sqrt{80} -8\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals to simplify $\\sqrt{80},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{80} & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{80}-8\\sqrt{5} &= 3(4\\sqrt{5})-8\\sqrt{5} \\\\ &= 12\\sqrt{5}-8\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{5}-8\\sqrt{5} &= (12-8)\\sqrt{5} \\\\ &= 4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832cc6f9-43ba-5a92-b599-63cd98874d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070092} {:answer/id #uuid "83d55fa4-28f6-5a90-b7c5-e9daa3ce1c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070093} {:answer/id #uuid "da7b9542-d0c9-5bb1-b3a6-5d4030d53b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070094} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb95b0c-a610-5642-8da6-75c961db2d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070095} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7176ab-d3cf-50d9-8828-f90e72fadd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7b9542-d0c9-5bb1-b3a6-5d4030d53b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070094} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb5b7f6-608c-58d4-b9fb-bb81516a054c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b18991a6-bb94-5a66-967b-9daa091157a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134363" :question/problem "$4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{12} & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12} &= 4\\sqrt{3}-5(2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ &= 4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3} &= (4-10)\\sqrt{3} \\\\ &= -6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078162 :question/id #uuid "5242547e-f74d-541d-8c3b-def606b70cb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1283" :question/problem "Evaluate the polynomial $x^3 +4x^2 -3$ at $x=-1$." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ into the polynomial and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{3} + 4x^{2} - 3 &= (-1)^{3} + 4(-1)^{2} - 3 \\\\ &= - 1 + 4(1) - 3 \\\\ &= - 1 + 4 - 3 \\\\ &= 0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078164 :question/id #uuid "7adb1612-0903-5a50-b5cd-75ebf1d0dd4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12854" :question/problem "Solve the equation $13 = \\dfrac{6y+8}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t13 &= \\dfrac{6y+8}{2} \\\\\n\t\t2 \\cdot 13 &= 2 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{6y+8}{2}\\right)  \\\\\n\t\t2 \\cdot 13 &= \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{6y+8}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) \\\\\n\t\t26 &= 6y+8\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t26 &= 6y+8 \\\\\n\t\t26-8 &= 6y+8-8 \\\\\n\t\t18 &= 6y \\\\\n\t\t\\dfrac{18}{6} &= \\dfrac{6y}{6} \\\\\n\t\t3 &= y\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=3.$"}]
